Z||000000||d825077||06-16-2000||08:46 PM||||Today when I came out to my car I noticed a three inch gash in the rear quarter panel.  Someone hit my car when it was parked and did not leave me any information.  How hard is it to prepair the car to be painted?  I got three estimates to fix it rainging from $440-$1000.  I figured if it was not to hard to fix the gash I could save a couple of dolars.||||reg||8
Z||000001||86SE||06-17-2000||12:13 AM||||I just bought a brand new quarter panel for $10.||||reg||14
Z||000002||TOM||06-17-2000||12:27 AM||||If it is a factory color then it is easy to fix cheap,||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||06-17-2000||12:37 PM||||Tom's right. If it's the factory color, it will be fairly easy to match.  Just go down to a paint supplier and buy about a pint? of the color you need and some clearcoat. Then you have to find someone that does a good paint job that would do it for free. Then, you could probably do the prepping yourself. Wet sanding, masking, etc. The paint guy would probably use a filler to fill in the gash.  Then he would sand it till it's even, then primer, paint, and clearcoat. Some bastard keyed my car, and I'm getting a guy to paint it for free! All I do is get the materials. Most would charge very little, as they don't do much work. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||d825077||06-17-2000||03:31 PM||||The color is the factory white.  What should I use to fill in the gash?  Is it hard to fill it in and make it smooth?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-17-2000||05:09 PM||||More importantly, is there anything you can use that is as flexible as the Enduroflex?||||reg||1
